{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>Progress shows that work is happening and, when you can, how far along
     it is. Pick the shape by context:</p>
  <table class="docs-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Shape</th><th>Use for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Linear</strong></td><td>In-flow waits tied to content — uploads, loading a list, a multi-step form</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Circle</strong></td><td>Standalone waits — page transitions, connecting states, spots too tight for a bar</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Determinate vs indeterminate</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Determinate</strong> whenever the fraction is knowable — a
        progress bar that moves honestly beats a spinner that guesses.</li>
    <li><strong>Indeterminate</strong> when duration is unknown — do not
        animate a fake percent toward an invented 100%.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>M2's consistency rules</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One type per activity</strong> — if a refresh shows a circle
        on one screen, the same action never shows a bar elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Sequences show overall progress</strong> — a multi-step
        process gets one indicator of the whole, not one per step.</li>
    <li><strong>Never decorative</strong> — an indicator that doesn't
        reflect a real process is noise; take it out.</li>
    <li><strong>In buttons: rare and short</strong> — a spinner inside an
        action is for short indeterminate waits (2–5s: "Connecting…").
        Don't attach progress to every button; and for longer waits, let a
        <a href="snackbar.html">snackbar</a> or notification carry the
        outcome instead.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "The state lives in the platform, not in classes",
    text: "Linear progress is the native <progress> element: value/max IS the state and :indeterminate (no value attribute) is the platform's own switch. The circle is a div — its state is one custom property, --fl-progress-value.",
    href: "snackbar.html",
    linkText: "Pair it with a snackbar"
  }) }}
  <p>A common pairing: an indeterminate spinner while connecting, then a
     determinate bar for the transfer, then a snackbar to confirm — three
     honest states, no invented numbers.</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <div class="docs-anatomy">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 190" role="img" aria-label="Progress anatomy diagram">
      <!-- linear -->
      <rect x="60" y="58" width="280" height="4" rx="2" fill="#e8f0fe"/>
      <rect x="60" y="58" width="140" height="4" rx="2" fill="#1a73e8"/>
      <!-- circle -->
      <circle cx="470" cy="60" r="20" fill="none" stroke="#e8f0fe" stroke-width="4"/>
      <path d="M 470 40 A 20 20 0 1 1 455.4 45.9" fill="none" stroke="#1a73e8" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="butt"/>
      <!-- callouts -->
      <g font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="700">
        <g><circle cx="50" cy="60" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="50" y="64" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">1</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="205" cy="40" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="205" y="44" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">2</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="515" cy="60" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="515" y="64" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">3</text></g>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Track</strong> — the tint (<code>--fl-tint</code>), 4px
          thick, 2px radius on the linear bar.</li>
      <li><strong>Fill</strong> — the accent (<code>--fl-accent</code>);
          width is the native <code>value</code> for linear, the arc is
          <code>--fl-progress-value</code> for the circle.</li>
      <li><strong>Circle ring</strong> — 4px stroke cut out of a
          conic-gradient disc with a mask; sized by
          <code>--fl-progress-size</code> (48px default, M2's circular
          spec). M2's circular track is invisible; Flint paints a faint
          tint ring so the scale reads at a glance — hide it per element
          with <code>style="--fl-tint: transparent"</code> for the strict
          M2 look.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zero JavaScript</strong> — the component ships no script at
        all. flint.js isn't involved; the page below runs with JS off
        entirely (the demo's only script is the author demo moving the
        value).</li>
    <li><strong>Linear determinate:</strong> set
        <code>value</code>/<code>max</code> — the native attributes. Moving
        the value moves the fill; the width change eases over
        <code>--fl-dur</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Linear indeterminate:</strong> remove the
        <code>value</code> attribute — <code>:indeterminate</code> swaps in
        a bar that grows and shrinks while sweeping the track (M2's
        choreography, one bar instead of two; 1.2s loop).</li>
    <li><strong>Circle determinate:</strong> set
        <code>--fl-progress-value: 0–100</code> (inline or from your JS).
        The ring is a conic-gradient — no JS library, no SVG arc math.</li>
    <li><strong>Circle indeterminate:</strong> add the
        <code>is-indeterminate</code> class — a 270° accent arc rotates
        (0.9s loop). M2's arc also grows and shrinks as it travels; Flint
        keeps a fixed arc — the same 2020-era idiom, one less moving
        part.</li>
    <li><strong>Sizing:</strong> <code>--fl-progress-size</code> scales the
        circle (stroke stays 4px); the linear bar is 4px tall and
        full-width of its container.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduced motion:</strong> the sweep stops and the bar parks
        at center; the spinning circle stops as a static 270° arc — state
        stays readable without the animation.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Linear:</strong> the native <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code>
        element is already a <code>progressbar</code> — just add
        <code>aria-label</code>. Don't add <code>role</code>; the platform
        has it.</li>
    <li><strong>Circle:</strong> a plain <code>div</code> knows nothing —
        give it <code>role="progressbar"</code>,
        <code>aria-valuemin/max/now</code> (determinate) or
        <code>aria-label</code> alone (indeterminate), and update
        <code>aria-valuenow</code> when <code>--fl-progress-value</code>
        changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Label the process, not the widget</strong> — "Uploading
        files", not "Progress".</li>
    <li><strong>Indeterminate is announced as such</strong> by both
        shapes — no fake percentages, matching the no-fake-100% rule
        above.</li>
    <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> the accent (#1a73e8) on the tint
        (#e8f0fe) track reads as a state indicator, not text — the honest
        pairing M2 uses; nearby text should carry the actual numbers.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

